# Public API Naming Conventions

## Boolean setters — `setX(bool)`

Use plain `setX(bool)` without an `Enabled` suffix.

```cpp
// ✅ GOOD
void setBasicAuth(bool enabled);
void setMergeAssets(bool enabled);
void setWebPConversion(bool enabled);

// ❌ BAD
void setBasicAuthEnabled(bool enabled);
void setMergeAssetsEnabled(bool enabled);
```

## Convenience enable methods — `enableX()`

Every `setX(bool)` that represents a feature toggle should have a matching `enableX()` shorthand. Implement it as a one-liner delegating to the setter.

```cpp
// Declaration (Geruest.hpp)
void enableBasicAuth();
void enableMergeAssets();
void enableWebPConversion();
void enableDevMode();

// Implementation (Geruest.cpp)
void Geruest::enableBasicAuth()     { setBasicAuth(true); }
void Geruest::enableMergeAssets()   { setMergeAssets(true); }
void Geruest::enableWebPConversion(){ setWebPConversion(true); }
```

## Feature activation — `enableX()`, not `activateX()`

Use the `enable` prefix consistently. Never use `activate`.

```cpp
// ✅ GOOD
void enableStatus(const std::string& token);

// ❌ BAD
void activateStatus(const std::string& token);
```
